ersonal care  lines and as you can see we have some of  the most world recognizable brands and  depending on where you are from in the  world some may be more recognizable than  others so let's quickly go through how  we built that new e-commerce e-commerce  site and for Colgate in a Google cloud  platform so we we had a challenge from  the business it was a challenge to  implement an e-commerce solution and an  S AP ecommerce site solution from start  to pilot in three months so we reviewed  our on-premise environment and found  that we didn't have the hardware  on-premise to support this  implementation and we also knew that the  hardware procurement cycle could take up  to two months just itself on Prem so we  had to be we also didn't know if it was  going to be a small medium or large  platform so that was what that meant we  had to find somewhere that was scalable  so GCP was the perfect fit so as part of  that we also had to integrate with our  as part of moving that application or  building that application in Google we  had to integrate with our current  on-premise environment so we had to lay  a foundation and part of that was  building the network connectivity using  using Google services such as cloud VPN  and cloud VPC we were able to build that  Network which gave us which allowed us  to build the application in Google and  and provide access to the storage and  images to run that application as well  as other back-end s ap applications what  was good it was that foundation was  built within a week and we were also  leveraging that foundation as part of  any new implementation in Google going  forward so it was one-stop shopping  so as a result after we built the  foundation we were able to stand up this  whole environment in one day by being  agile with by standing up this  environment we provided this environment  to our to our application teams and then  they were able to produce to install the  application and produce testing cycles  within a few days after that so as a  result our IT organization was able to  meet our business goals and we provided  a future roadmap for delivering rapid  solutions to the business so future  state we'd like to be able to beyond be  even more agile by using third-party  application third-party providers such  as terraform to leverage infrastructure  as a code and be more agile going  forward so let me hand you over to  Vinnie who's going to take you over  through our migration project so again  my name is Vincent Richie I am the lead  cloud engineer at Colgate Palmolive and  today we're going to go over some of the  foundational elements we had to put in  place prior to migrating to GCP so the  scope of the project was simple on paper  we had to migrate a workload of 1,200  Windows machines and stay within the  lift and shift migration strategy we  wanted to avoid any rear connecting or  Reap platforming as anywhere possible  that way we could keep our targeted  goals in place early on we partnered  with Google's professional services  organization they helped us move along  that journey to migrate to GCP we do  this in a couple different ways so what  we did in the beginning is we set up a  bunch of in-depth of workshops with our  on-premise support teams that gave us  the ability to identify how Colgate  operates on a day to day from their  Google was able to produce a technical  design document and that became our  true roadmap or our blueprint going  forward on how we're actually going to  migrate to GCP now within that TDD it  was broken up into sections of the  project and that those sections were  called work streams we're gonna go over  them in a second so the work streams  were broken up into four key areas  building the foundation was a key part  of our project it allowed us to build  all the technical building blocks that  were needed before we migrated to GCP  second we migrated a pilot workload  this gave our teams the early access to  understand how a migration works and  then in parallel we also implemented a  data data center discovery tool that  analyzed all of our connections within  our data center so we can understand  what servers are communicating to others  and then our final final work stream was  our full Windows workload migration so  for this presentation we're actually  going to focus on the first and third  work streams so let's take a little  deeper into those foundational elements  I spoke about earlier so the foundation  really consisted of three parts cloud  governance understanding what governance  policies we needed to continue to do in  GCP while also building new policies  that were more cloud focused building a  landing zone so this is the the  technical building blocks I spoke about  earlier and all the pieces that were  part of that in order to migrate to TCP  and then we needed to bring in some new  tools and these tools were gonna help us  go along that journey so in the area of  cloud governance a couple questions were  asked early on such as which servers  could be migrated and how are we going  to manage them how are we going to  secure them and how are we going to  control cost so in the area of cloud  readiness we had to come up with a way  to identify how we had to come up with a  way to identify that our server  were ready so a couple examples  understanding how the license structure  of an application could translate from  on-premise to cloud understanding if the  applications support from the vendor  carried over the same level of support  that we had on-premise that that was a  key part  prior to migrating to TCP also  understanding the amount of  communication that servers were having  to each other on premise also was  important to understand if they were  cloud ready in the area of  infrastructure management what we had to  do there is understand that we were  going to continue to use some of our  on-premise applications in the areas of  patch management and load balancing and  monitoring and we identified this  because we wanted to ensure that we  didn't have a reduced functionality of  what we currently have but also we  wanted to also continue to leverage our  on-premise or our companies teams  expertise and the tools that they  already know now on the flip side to  that we are also continuing to  investigate more cloud focused solutions  to help us along our long-term journey  platform security so Colgate is a G  suite company so being able to integrate  with our current G suite implementation  we created custom custom groups that  made access to our Google platform for  our support teams quick and efficient  but then for our users we were able to  use the power of I M or Identity and  Access Management to give our users the  ability to be agile in the cloud but  also control some more sensitive areas  that we needed more control over and  then finally cost management we had to  come up with a way that once we migrated  to GCP how are we going to manage cost  so we did that in a couple different  ways by by exporting our billing  information from GCP to bigquery  we were able to leverage tools such as  data studio to build custom dashboards  to give us the needed visibility into  our costs for our projects  and then an area of control we used  billing alerts within the GCP console to  notify us that when projects reach a  certain overage in terms of their cost  structure so I want to go a little bit  deeper into the network architecture and  what we had to do to to be ready for a  cloud we knew early on that we had to  keep connectivity back to on-prem with  our with our cloud environment that  hybrid connectivity so and we also  wanted to make sure that we set up our  isolated networks in a way that we were  as secure as possible so originally we  set up we did this through multiple V  pcs for both are non prod and production  environments we also configured VPNs for  that hybrid connectivity to multiple  regions in the cloud that way we could  leverage that global GCP network and  then in a high in our higher traffic  areas we implemented interconnects to  ensure that that bandwidth has increased  now for our DCP communication we  implemented shared V pcs to enable our  projects within the same environment to  communicate to each other but in doing  so we also implemented micro  segmentation that way servers could  communicate to other servers only when  we allow it even if it's sitting on  within the same subnet that gave us the  highest level of security moving forward  so let's talk about some of the tools  that we're using to help us with our  migration journey so a division is an  agentless discovery tool that we  currently have deployed in our data  center it is helping us map our entire  server footprint of our data center and  it's doing that through a couple key  it's providing us a couple key things in  terms of technical details so as we as I  said server-to-server communication but  how often is that server communicating  to that other server right that could  help us identify some of our  talkers within our data center and as  they are communicating to each other  what kind of protocols are they using  TCP UDP is it inbound outbound traffic  what ports are they using right so  implementing something like micro  segmentation you need to have all that  information prior to migrating to GCP at  the end of it having that data center  footprint allowed us to create something  called migration move groups and what  that basically means is servers that  should move together get moved together  so for cloud security and compliance we  are leveraging security command center  along with for steady security and what  that's basically allowing us to do is  put in all the necessary rules in place  to make our GCP environment more secure  once those rules are in place we're not  only able to sniff out vulnerabilities  of areas in GCP but prevent them from  happening in the first place other areas  such as organizational policies that  allows us the highest level of controls  to be put in place that are then  hierarchy down to all of our service  projects so I want to talk a little bit  about server migration um  the tool that we are currently using is  called Bella strata so once your hybrid  connectivity is in place from your  on-prem environment to your data center  you basically have four major steps that  you need to do when using the Velo scrub  tool so step one would be to implement  your management management pieces of El  Estrada so what that is is an appliance  in your data center and that is directly  working with your VMware environment and  then you have your compute instances  running in the GCP side now these two  pieces of Bella strata are in constant  sync with each other they'll they allow  basically the entire migration process  that we're going to talk about on this  slide - to occur so once your management  pieces are in place we could begin -  migration so on Krim within your your V  Center VMware environment you're able to  exit  migration and what that's doing is it's  going to take the server configuration  that you have on Prem and replicate it  in the GCP environment but you do have  the option to change that configuration  based on different needs  maybe that server on Prem was sized at a  higher workload that it needed to be  within a short time 30 minutes or so the  GCP instances are accessible so that's a  key point here because you don't have to  have an excessive amount of downtime you  could turn over these instances to your  support teams or your application teams  to begin additional configuration or to  just begin testing of that application  in GCP in the meantime storage migration  migration begins  so the on-premise disks will then start  to begin the streaming of the move the  migrating of storage to new disks within  the GCP side now depending on the size  of those disks will correlate to the  length of that disk migration once a  storage migration is finished you're  basically ready to do your last step and  that step is called a detach and it's  attached is basically disconnecting your  on-premise environment from your DCP  environment in terms of El Estrada and  those disks that are now fully migrated  are now attached to those instances and  you're done that that in a nutshell is  your four steps on how to migrate your  on-premise servers to GCP I have a  couple slides here just to wrap up my  part of the presentation just want to  talk about some integration points to  consider when possibly migrating your  environment to DCP so in terms of  firewall management going to a DCP  environment or public cloud environment  you may want to consider a more granular  granular firewall management such as  micro segmentation but when doing so it  is critical to understand how your  on-premise environment is communicating  like I mentioned from our conversation  before about a division in terms of  patch management we might want to  consider a new patch management strategy  when going to the cloud this is a great  time to think about  that having a having a pre-migration a  detailed pre-migration plan where you  could identify some of the things that  might get missed during a migration is  key so yes we have our a division data  that's our technical data but being able  to supplement it with some something  that we do such as application  interviews where we speak to the  application owners and get some insights  that may be a tool can't give us so  being able to take that technical detail  and that app owner detail put it  together gives us a higher chance or  high confidence when migrating to GCP so  we don't miss something let's say an  application that might be leveraging a  load balancer IP address mapping so when  we are implementing hybrid environment  where you're connecting your GCP  environment to your on-premise  environment you want to make sure that  those subnets that you're provisioning  and GCP don't conflict with your subnets  that you already have within your data  center or globally for your company on  the application side you want to make  sure that you understand how your  applications are currently interfacing  on-premise that's going to be key to  understand how you're going to move not  just one application but many  applications at a time to keep those  move groups together you also want to  make sure that your external external  storage that your applications may use  stays intact  post migration and then having a very  good detailed cloud compatibility check  that's that cloud readiness could help  you avoid some potential issues in the  future let's talk about some post  migration benefits so a lot of benefits  that you hear usually come around a net  new environment so I mean someone is  building that new and these are all the  benefits that you get from running in  the cloud but a lot of those benefits  also correlate over to someone that's  migrating their servers to the cloud so  live migration all right being able to  reduce your potentially reduce your  maintenance windows by not having to  patch the physical hardware because the  live migration is  to the customer right sizing leveraging  Google's recommendation engine and  artificial intelligence to understand  when servers are underutilized or over  utilized and then provision the correct  sizing for those servers something even  as simple as hardware leases right when  migrating to the cloud you no longer  have to worry about the hardware leases  and managing those schedules fast their  provisioning not having to worry about  compassed the capacity planning which  every datacenter has to worry about and  and using the agility and the  scalability of the cloud platform to  provision faster and basically on-demand  and then finally reduce license  management so leveraging Google's images  such as Windows and sequel server you no  longer have to worry about those  licenses and managing those licenses  costs because they are built into the  into the build itself so in a nutshell  GCP is helping us as a company to become  more agile google's professional  services are continuing to help us grow  in that sector and then the tools that  were using are helping us to get to our  destination  [Music]
 

